---
// firma2-measure-reporter-preview — the ARTIFACT panel of a performance
// measure's setup page: the form somebody will actually fill in to report this
// measure, and, beside it, what the system fills in unasked.
//
// SOMEBODY, not "the project", because that depends on the measure's KIND. An
// output is filed by the project that did the work; an outcome is filed by a
// monitoring effort about a place, and saying otherwise on this panel would
// teach an admin the wrong model of their own data.
//
// WHY AN ARTIFACT PANEL AT ALL. Every other section on this page collects the
// DEFINITION — name, quantity, counting rule, dimensions. None of them shows
// what the definition PRODUCES. What a measure definition produces is a form
// somebody has to fill in, several times a season, and the admin authoring the
// definition never sees it. This panel is the only place they do.
//
// WHAT IT USED TO SHOW, AND WHY THAT WAS WRONG. The previous version crossed
// every subcategory with every other and drew the cartesian product as a filled
// matrix — "16 values per reporting period", with an alert box when the grid got
// big. Reporting is not a filled grid. It is SPARSE ROWS: a project that only
// ran broadcast burns on federal ground files ONE entry, and the fifteen other
// cells never existed. The matrix took a cheap feature and made it look
// punishing, so it is gone — product, cell count, alarm and all.
//
// WHAT IT SHOWS NOW IS A CONTRAST, and the contrast IS the design argument. A
// dimension declares a SOURCE (see the data module's header): `reported` means a
// person picks it, `spatial` / `historical` / `record` mean the system derives
// it for free. So the panel is two blocks with a rule between them — the
// questions a reporter is asked, then what arrives without being asked — drawn
// as opposites so the difference survives a glance: entry answers sit in sunken
// wells (a slot someone fills), derived answers are plain text (already known).
// Below them both, once, the trade as a datum: questions per entry against
// dimensions on the report.
//
// THE FORM IT DRAWS FORKS WITH THE MEASURE'S KIND, which is the point of having
// a preview at all — an admin who picked "outcome" in the create dialog finds
// out here what that answer bought them. An OUTPUT opens with "what did you
// do": the primary reported dimension, then the place, the amount, and the
// dimensions that qualify it. An OUTCOME has no such question, because nobody
// DID a survival rate; it is a reading, so it opens with WHERE and WHEN and its
// reported dimensions qualify the reading instead. The lower block forks with
// it: an output's spatial rows ALLOCATE the quantity (18 acres inside, 2
// outside), an outcome's classify the monitoring location and carry no amounts,
// because a rate is not made of parts. The two forms are visibly different
// objects, which is what makes the fork something an admin can trust rather
// than a flag on a record. All of that shape lives in the renderer (see below);
// this file frames it and never decides it.
//
// AND IT IS DELIVERED WHILE THE ADMIN IS STILL DECIDING. This panel was a pure
// build-time render until it wasn't: the admin changed a dimension's source in
// the config rail and the panel stayed on the previous answer. A preview that
// reports the state of the record as of the last deploy is worse than no
// preview, because it is confidently wrong at exactly the moment it is being
// consulted. It re-renders from the working record on MEASURE_CHANGE_EVENT (see
// the script at the foot of the file).
//
// THE MARKUP IS NOT IN THIS FILE. It is in ../../lib/measure-reporter-preview.ts,
// with the model that feeds it, and both this component and the client script
// call the SAME renderer. Writing the panel twice — once in the template for the
// server render, once in a script for the update — is the one failure mode a
// change like this has, and it is silent: the copies stay identical until the
// first fix lands in one of them. Read that module's header for the rest of the
// reasoning, including why the spatial split is drawn as an allocation and how
// its numbers are derived rather than rolled; everything below is the frame
// around its output.
//
// SUBTRACTIVE. This panel shows only what a REPORTER would meet: the measure's
// name, the guidance, the questions, the derived values. It does not restate the
// definition, the program, the counting rule, the status or the project count —
// sibling cards own all of those, and a second copy here would be the same fact
// twice on one screen.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: the value cells, the two label/value lists and the
// allocation list are hand-built, and each needed the lookup.
//   (1) THE VALUE CELLS ARE NOT esa-text-field / esa-select, ON PURPOSE.
//       Those are the right legos for a field a person operates. Nothing here is
//       one. See the INERT note below — a preview made of real (even disabled)
//       controls is a preview that lies about itself and litters the tab order.
//   (2) NO TABLE, AND NO GRID LEGO EITHER. Checked the full
//       node_modules/@esa/ecology/src/components catalog: there is no
//       table/description-list lego, and esa-grid is a documentation page for
//       Beacon's Angular AG Grid wrapper with no .astro or .ts to import. AG
//       Grid — this spoke's established tabular surface — is the wrong tool
//       twice over: this is not tabular data any more (the matrix is what got
//       deleted), it is a handful of label/value pairs, and shipping a grid
//       runtime to draw eight rows would cost more than the page it decorates.
//       A <dl> is the correct primitive: these ARE terms and their values, and
//       a two-column CSS grid over dt/dd is the standard way to align one. It
//       is not a reinvented layout primitive — layouts.css's `.grid` is a
//       responsive auto-fit card track, a different job — and the primitives
//       that DO fit are used as-is: `.stack` for the blocks, `.cluster` for the
//       allocation parts and the cost line.
//   (3) THE MARKUP IS BUILT AS A STRING, which the component-first rule treats
//       with the same suspicion as bespoke CSS, and correctly. The one lego this
//       panel uses is still a lego, and is NOT reproduced in a template literal:
//       esa-collapsible frames the panel here, in the template, and the renderer
//       only ever writes inside it.
//   (4) esa-alert-box IS GONE, not replaced. It carried the large-grid alarm,
//       and the alarm was the misrepresentation this rewrite removes. Nothing
//       about a measure's shape warrants an alert now: a measure with six
//       dimensions and two questions is the GOOD case.
// Beacon is not cloned on this machine, so tier 2 is unavailable.
//
// THE FIELDS ARE INERT BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY ATTRIBUTE — still the single most
// important call in this file, and the rewrite does not get to erode it. Three
// mechanisms were on the table:
//   - `disabled` controls. Removed from the tab order, but they render in the
//     disabled palette, which in this design system means "this control is
//     unavailable to you right now" — a temporary, fixable state. That is a
//     different claim from "this is a picture of somebody else's form", and it
//     also collides with the house rule against greying dense text out.
//   - `inert` on a wrapper of real esa-text-fields and esa-selects. Correct
//     semantics, but it boots a Lit custom element per row purely as decoration,
//     and an `inert` subtree is invisible to assistive tech — a screen-reader
//     user would be told the panel is empty, when in fact its shape is exactly
//     the information they came for.
//   - What is built here: NON-INTERACTIVE CELLS. Each answer is a <span> in a
//     sunken well rather than a control's raised fill. Zero tab stops, zero
//     controls, nothing to submit, no caret glyph borrowed from a select — and
//     every cell still reads to a screen reader as real content inside a
//     properly associated <dl>, so the shape of the form survives non-visually.
//   The panel now has NO tab stop at all (the old horizontal scroller went with
//   the matrix), which is why the update path below needs no focus restoration:
//   there is nothing inside it focus can be sitting on.
//
// THE WORKED NUMBERS ARE LABELLED AS SUCH. Showing an allocation requires a
// quantity to allocate, so this preview carries example values where the old one
// carried format masks. That is a real risk the old design did not have — an
// admin reading "Federal 20 acres" as a figure from a live project has been
// misled — so the renderer prints one short line saying it is an example, and
// that same line names who files the real one ("the project", or "a monitoring
// effort, about a place", read straight off measureKind()). The numbers
// themselves are deterministic and derived from the record (see the module
// header): no Math.random(), no clock, identical on every build.
//
// DEGRADES ON A BLANK DRAFT, DELIBERATELY — AND THE TWO BLANKS DIFFER. There is
// one empty draft per kind, because the create dialog routes on the fork, and
// they do not render alike:
//   - `draft-untitled-output` has nothing decided and therefore asks nothing.
//     A titled panel, the cost line reading zero, and NOTHING under it: no
//     heading over an empty list, no empty state, no "complete the definition
//     first" gate.
//   - `draft-untitled-outcome` still asks WHERE and WHEN, under "Add reading",
//     and its cost line reads two questions against zero dimensions. Those two
//     are not authored dimensions the draft is missing — they are the frame of
//     any reading, so an outcome has them before its author has typed a word.
// Both are honest previews of a measure with nothing decided, and both look
// deliberate rather than broken. A measure you have not finished is the normal
// state of this page rather than an error; the readiness card next door already
// owns what is outstanding.
import EsaCollapsible from '@esa/ecology/esa-collapsible.astro';
import type { PerformanceMeasureDefinition } from '../../data/firma2-performance-measures';
import {
  buildReporterPreviewModel,
  renderReporterPreview,
  PREVIEW_COUNT_ATTR,
  PREVIEW_PANEL_TITLE,
  PREVIEW_ROOT_ATTR,
} from '../../lib/measure-reporter-preview';

interface Props {
  /** The measure being set up. Every field may be unset — this is a draft. */
  measure: PerformanceMeasureDefinition;
}

const { measure } = Astro.props;

// The build-time render. The client script calls these same two lines on every
// change; nothing about the shape is decided here.
const model = buildReporterPreviewModel(measure);
const bodyHtml = renderReporterPreview(model);
---

{/*
  A plain <section> for the anchor, with no accessible name. It carries an id so
  in-page navigation can reach the panel, and stays role=generic on purpose:
  this page already stacks several named card sections, and one more landmark
  whose name repeats the disclosure summary two lines below it adds a stop to
  the landmark list without adding a destination.
*/}
<section id="reporter-preview">
  {/*
    Bordered, not `flush`. The flush variant exists for stacked drawer sections
    separated by the host's own dividers; this panel sits on the setup page
    beside the esa-card sections, and an unframed block among framed ones reads
    as something that failed to load. `open` because a preview nobody opens is a
    preview nobody reads — the whole value of this panel is that the trade is on
    screen before the admin leaves the page.

    THE DISCLOSURE IS NEVER RE-RENDERED. esa-collapsible is a native
    <details open>, so its open/closed state is an attribute on an element the
    update path does not touch: the script writes into the container INSIDE the
    body and nothing above it. A panel the admin collapsed stays collapsed while
    they keep editing, and one they opened does not snap shut on the next
    keystroke — which is what re-rendering the whole panel would have done.
  */}
  <EsaCollapsible title={PREVIEW_PANEL_TITLE} icon="file-text" open>
    {/*
      ONE container, which is both the styling root and the write target. The
      previous version needed three (head, alert, body) so an esa-alert-box
      instance could survive between them; with the alarm deleted there is no
      lego inside the render area, so there is nothing an innerHTML swap can
      destroy — and one container is one place for a future reader to look.
    */}
    <div
      class="firma2-reporter-preview stack"
      data-gap="md"
      {...{ [PREVIEW_ROOT_ATTR]: measure.slug, [PREVIEW_COUNT_ATTR]: String(model.questionCount) }}
      set:html={bodyHtml}
    />
  </EsaCollapsible>
</section>

<script>
  import { announce } from '@esa/ecology/announcer';
  import { MEASURE_CHANGE_EVENT, type MeasureChangeDetail } from '../../lib/measure-draft';
  import {
    buildReporterPreviewModel,
    renderReporterPreview,
    PREVIEW_COUNT_ATTR,
    PREVIEW_PANEL_TITLE,
    PREVIEW_ROOT_ATTR,
    PREVIEW_ROOT_SELECTOR,
  } from '../../lib/measure-reporter-preview';

  // THE SECOND CALLER. Everything this script draws comes out of the same render
  // function the frontmatter above used; the only thing it owns is WHEN and
  // WHERE, plus the questions a re-render raises that a build does not: what
  // happens to focus, what happens to the scroll position, and what a screen
  // reader user who is nowhere near this panel is told.
  //
  // The first two answer themselves here. The panel contains no focusable
  // element and no scroller, so an innerHTML swap cannot drop focus to <body>
  // and cannot lose a scroll offset. Nothing in this script moves focus or
  // scrolls: the user is typing in the config rail and stays there.

  // Queried by attribute rather than by id: nothing stops a screen from stacking
  // two of these panels, and an id would make the second one silently inert.
  // The attribute carries the slug, so a panel only answers for its own record.
  const roots = () => document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(PREVIEW_ROOT_SELECTOR);

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------
  // SC 4.1.3 — why this announces the trade and nothing else
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // The panel changes while the user's focus is in the config rail, so this is a
  // status change, not a change of context. Working the accessibility skill's
  // order top-down:
  //
  //   1. An instructional cue. The right primary mechanism, and it belongs on
  //      the controls that CAUSE the change — "a reported dimension adds a
  //      question to every entry; a derived one does not" as an aria-describedby
  //      in the rail. This component does not own the rail, so it cannot place
  //      it; see the note at the foot of this file. A cue would reduce what
  //      follows, not replace it: it sets an expectation, it does not report the
  //      resulting number.
  //   2. Moving focus. Rejected outright. Yanking focus out of a text field
  //      mid-word to show someone a form preview is a worse bug than the one
  //      being fixed, and the user did not ask to go anywhere.
  //   3. An ARIA state property. There is none. No attribute expresses "this
  //      entry now asks four questions", and aria-busy would be a lie — nothing
  //      is loading.
  //   4. So: the announcer, under three hard guards.
  //
  // The guards are the whole design, because the failure mode here is not
  // silence — it is a region that reads the entire form back on every keystroke
  // of the guidance textarea until the user turns speech off.
  //
  //   GUARD 1 — ONE FACT. Only the cost line is announced, never the form. Under
  //     the new model that line is "N questions per entry, M dimensions on the
  //     report": the trade this panel exists to deliver, and the one thing that
  //     cannot be read off any other surface on the page. The fields and the
  //     derived values are content, and content is reviewable at the panel —
  //     which is why it is a real <dl> with real labels, and not a live region.
  //   GUARD 2 — TRANSITIONS ONLY. Nothing is said unless the sentence actually
  //     changed from the last thing said. Typing a name, a quantity word or
  //     guidance, or switching the counting rule, redraws the panel in total
  //     silence. Naming a dimension that has no source yet is also silent.
  //     Changing a dimension's source from reported to spatial does speak — it
  //     moves a question off the form, which is the entire point of the model.
  //   GUARD 3 — QUIET PERIOD. Nine hundred milliseconds after the last change,
  //     not on each one. Adding three dimensions in a row walks the count; the
  //     user hears the answer, once, not the working.
  //
  // Polite, never assertive: nothing was rejected and nothing failed, so this
  // waits for a pause instead of stopping a sentence the user is in the middle
  // of hearing. The message names the panel with the same string the disclosure
  // shows, so it arrives with a referent and points at somewhere real.
  const ANNOUNCE_QUIET_MS = 900;

  // Renders coalesce too, at a shorter delay. Not for CPU — the DOM here is a
  // few dozen nodes — but because each render swaps a subtree out from under the
  // screen reader's virtual buffer, and doing that on every keystroke churns it
  // for no benefit. A tenth of a second still reads as instant to the eye.
  const RENDER_DEBOUNCE_MS = 100;

  const announced = new WeakMap<HTMLElement, string>();
  const renderTimers = new WeakMap<HTMLElement, number>();
  const announceTimers = new WeakMap<HTMLElement, number>();

  const render = (root: HTMLElement, measure: MeasureChangeDetail['measure']): void => {
    const model = buildReporterPreviewModel(measure);

    root.innerHTML = renderReporterPreview(model);
    root.setAttribute(PREVIEW_COUNT_ATTR, String(model.questionCount));

    // GUARD 2 + GUARD 3. The baseline is whatever the build rendered, captured
    // the first time through, so the initial state never announces itself.
    if (!announced.has(root)) announced.set(root, model.costSummary);
    window.clearTimeout(announceTimers.get(root));
    announceTimers.set(
      root,
      window.setTimeout(() => {
        if (announced.get(root) === model.costSummary) return;
        announced.set(root, model.costSummary);
        announce(`${PREVIEW_PANEL_TITLE}: ${model.costSummary}`);
      }, ANNOUNCE_QUIET_MS),
    );
  };

  document.addEventListener(MEASURE_CHANGE_EVENT, (event) => {
    const { measure } = (event as CustomEvent<MeasureChangeDetail>).detail;
    if (!measure) return;
    roots().forEach((root) => {
      if (root.getAttribute(PREVIEW_ROOT_ATTR) !== measure.slug) return;
      window.clearTimeout(renderTimers.get(root));
      renderTimers.set(
        root,
        window.setTimeout(() => render(root, measure), RENDER_DEBOUNCE_MS),
      );
    });
  });

  // NOTE FOR WHOEVER OWNS THE CONFIG RAIL. The one mechanism above this in the
  // status-message order that this file cannot reach is the instructional cue,
  // because it hangs off the control that causes the change: the dimension
  // editor. A visually-hidden line wired with aria-describedby — that a reported
  // dimension adds a question to the entry form preview below, and a derived one
  // does not — would tell a screen-reader user once, up front, what a sighted
  // user infers from watching the two blocks trade rows. It is additive to the
  // announcement, not a replacement: the cue says the panel moves, the
  // announcement says where it landed.
</script>

<style is:global>
  /* GLOBAL, NOT SCOPED, AND ONLY BECAUSE OF set:html. Astro scopes a component's
     CSS by stamping a data-astro-cid-* attribute onto the elements in its
     TEMPLATE — and markup delivered through `set:html`, or written by the script
     above, never gets stamped. Scoped rules would therefore match the build-time
     render and silently stop matching the moment the panel updated: an unstyled
     list appearing on the first edit, with nothing in the console. Every
     selector below is namespaced `firma2-reporter-preview*`, which is the
     containment this trades the scoper for. */

  /* The form's own heading. `title` rather than the card ramp's
     `title-sm-strong`, because inside the panel this is the top of a document,
     not a section label in a stack of them. */
  .firma2-reporter-preview__form-title {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-content-default, #202020);
  }

  .firma2-reporter-preview__note {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #646464);
  }

  .firma2-reporter-preview__cost {
    margin: 0;
    /* The cluster's own knob rather than an align-items override: the cost is
       set in a strong label role and the yield beside it in body, so centring
       them would float the smaller one off the shared baseline. */
    --align: baseline;
  }

  /* The cost is what an author is trading away and reads at full content colour;
     the yield beside it is what they get, and steps back one so the pair reads
     as one line rather than two competing figures. */
  .firma2-reporter-preview__cost-figure {
    color: var(--color-content-default, #202020);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .firma2-reporter-preview__cost-yield,
  .firma2-reporter-preview__sep {
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #646464);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* Both blocks are section-level labels inside the panel: normal case, 16px,
     full content colour. Not an eyebrow, not letter-spaced, not uppercase. */
  .firma2-reporter-preview__block-title {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-content-default, #202020);
  }

  /* THE RULE BETWEEN THE TWO BLOCKS — a hairline across the panel, because the
     whole argument is the contrast either side of it. A border, not a colour
     wash or a tinted panel: the container stays neutral and the difference is
     carried by the CONTENT (wells above, plain text below). */
  .firma2-reporter-preview__block--derived {
    border-block-start: var(--border-width-default, 1px) solid
      var(--color-border-default-subtle, #e4e4e4);
    padding-block-start: var(--spacing-400, 1rem);
  }

  /* Guidance is the only prose a reporter reads before typing, so it holds the
     16px body role and full content colour. It is not helper text to be skimmed
     past — it is the rule the number has to satisfy. */
  .firma2-reporter-preview__guidance {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-content-default, #202020);
  }

  /* THE LABEL/VALUE GRID. Two columns over the <dl>'s own dt/dd children — no
     wrapper divs, no display:contents — so every row aligns on one label column
     without a table. fit-content caps that column at 12rem: a long dimension
     name wraps inside it instead of squeezing the answers off the right. */
  .firma2-reporter-preview__rows {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: fit-content(12rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: var(--spacing-400, 1rem);
    row-gap: var(--spacing-200, 0.5rem);
    align-items: baseline;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* DARKENED, NOT GREYED. Labels sit at the 14px dense floor, so they take the
     full content colour — the rule is that text gets darker as it gets smaller,
     never lighter. The well beside them, not a colour step, is what separates a
     label from its answer. */
  .firma2-reporter-preview__label {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-content-default, #202020);
  }

  .firma2-reporter-preview__value {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-content-default, #202020);
    /* The unit sits beside the well on the same baseline, and an allocation
       wraps part by part rather than clipping. */
    --align: baseline;
  }

  /* THE WELL. This is what an answer looks like when it is not a control:
     sunken rather than the raised fill esa-text-field paints, no caret, no
     hover, no focus ring, nothing to click, no dropdown chevron borrowed from a
     select. It reads unmistakably as "an answer goes here" and equally
     unmistakably as "not by you". */
  .firma2-reporter-preview__well {
    display: inline-block;
    min-width: 9rem;
    padding: var(--spacing-150, 0.375rem) var(--spacing-200, 0.5rem);
    border: var(--border-width-default, 1px) solid var(--color-border-default-subtle, #e4e4e4);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm, 0.25rem);
    background: var(--color-background-elevation-sunken, #f4f4f4);
    color: var(--color-content-default, #202020);
  }

  /* The quantity is the one answer that is a number, so its well is sized for
     one — a nine-rem slot around "20" would read as a text box for a sentence.
     4rem holds five figures at the dense role. */
  .firma2-reporter-preview__well--quantity {
    min-width: 4rem;
    text-align: end;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* The unit is printed ON the form, not typed into it, so it sits outside the
     well — the same place it sits on a real entry row. */
  .firma2-reporter-preview__unit {
    color: var(--color-content-default, #202020);
  }

  /* DERIVED VALUES ARE PLAIN TEXT. No well, no border, no fill: there is no slot
     to fill because the answer is already in. That absence is the panel's whole
     argument, drawn rather than narrated. */
  .firma2-reporter-preview__parts {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .firma2-reporter-preview__part {
    display: inline-flex;
    gap: var(--spacing-100, 0.25rem);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  /* AN ALLOCATION IS SEPARATED, NOT PUNCTUATED. The middot is generated, so it
     never lands in the text a screen reader reads back — the <li> boundaries
     already say where one share ends and the next begins. */
  .firma2-reporter-preview__part + .firma2-reporter-preview__part::before {
    content: '·';
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #646464);
  }

  /* The share of the quantity that fell in this value. Tabular figures so two
     shares line up as a pair of numbers, which is what makes the row read as a
     split of one total rather than two unrelated facts. */
  .firma2-reporter-preview__part-amount {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* NARROW PANEL — the two-column grid leaves neither column usable below about
     26rem, so the label stacks over its answer, which is how a real form would
     fold anyway.

     A CONTAINER QUERY, NOT A MEDIA QUERY, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A REAL BUG THIS
     FIXES. This used to be `@media (max-width: 30rem)`, which asks about the
     VIEWPORT. That was accidentally fine while this panel took the page's wide
     column: a 30rem viewport meant a 30rem panel. It stopped being fine the day
     the setup page moved the panel into a 24rem rail — the viewport is 1440px
     there, so the query never fires, and the grid stays two-column inside a box
     too narrow to hold it. `fit-content(12rem)` plus the well's own `min-width:
     9rem` comes to more than 22rem of usable rail, and `minmax(0, 1fr)` zeroes
     the track's automatic minimum, so the well OVERFLOWS its panel rather than
     wrapping. A container query asks about the box the rule is actually about.

     26rem rather than 30rem because the box is now the panel itself rather than
     the whole window, so the threshold no longer has to leave room for a rail
     beside it. */
  .firma2-reporter-preview {
    container-type: inline-size;
  }

  @container (max-width: 26rem) {
    /* THE COST LINE'S SEPARATOR GOES WHEN THE LINE WRAPS. The two facts are
       separate spans in a `.cluster` precisely so the yield can drop under the
       cost in a narrow rail — that part works. What the markup could not know is
       that the `·` is its own flex item, so it stays on line one and strands
       there: "4 questions per entry ·" over "6 breakdowns on the report". A
       separator with nothing after it reads as truncation. It is decoration and
       already aria-hidden, so once the two facts are on separate lines the line
       break IS the separation and the glyph has no job left. */
    .firma2-reporter-preview__sep {
